        <groundspeak:log id="249304163">
          <groundspeak:log_wpt lat="55.509617" lon="-115.911283" />
          <groundspeak:date>2012-07-11T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="985002">Strider&#39;s Ride</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP478 Cut Bank Point&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After visiting the cap at Shaw's Point, we headed east down the gravel road looking for a good spot to access the cap at Cut Bank. The decision was made after a drive down the road to head directly to the cap via a straight line through the bush. The hike down to the cap was a warm one with all the gear being worn for bug protection combined with 30+ C temperatures, but at least it was done mostly in the shade of the trees. It was really satisfying to knock this one off the list... and then quickly retreat back to the vehicle for some cool refreshments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;TFTCap ofth&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="248786317">
          <groundspeak:log_wpt lat="55.509617" lon="-115.911233" />
          <groundspeak:date>2012-07-11T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="321730">Sleepy_hollow</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP478 - Cut Bank Point&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After our pleasant excursion to Shaw's Point we decided to check out Cut Bank Point a few kilometres to the east. We picked up the rough gravel road that led to Hilliard's Bay and followed it east to see how close it might get to Cut Bank Point. After driving along the road, the closest access distance we could find was 1.7 km.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So we got all of our gear together (including our Deluxe Bug Screen Hats!) for the short trip through the forest to the cap site. We worked our way through some fairly thick bush attempting to stay high along the ridge as long as possible and to potentially avoid the crossing of any drainages we might encounter. Unfortunately on our route we encountered two fair sized drainage crossings (thankfully dry) and had no difficulty making the crossings.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Once near the cap site we spotted three GPs and a few minutes later the pristine cap. The area of the cap site is thick bush with little of no view of the near by lake. &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Overall our track showed a round trip distance of 3.65 km through some of the thickest bush possible. Needless to say - the Deluxe Bug Screen Hats are a real necessity in the north and especially in this area. Thankfully we had no problem with the skeeters and horse flies thanks to our careful preparation, but the heat and humidity definitely made the trip a real challenge. Thanks OFTH for an interesting cap hunt a little off the beaten trail!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="247871669">
          <groundspeak:date>2012-07-06T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="836983">Coolcat21</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP478 Cut Bank Point?

Posted: N55 30.577  W115 54.674

?GPSr: N55 30.576  W115 54.676?

Elevation: 601m&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It was a memorable trek along a section of the Trans Canada Trail to this one. What I found strange is as I made my way along the road toward the trailhead, the evidence of human activity and habitation actually increased. None of this showed up on the GE pics I was looking at when reconning the hike. But that stops abruptly near the trailhead. I parked near N 55° 30.655 W 115° 57.998 and started out at the well marked trailhead.  The trail looks a bit overgrown in spots but there is next to no deadfall crossing the trail so it's very easy to keep good time as you walk along. This hike was definitely the highpoint of the day.  &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The only negative thing I have to mention, and of course its a seasonal thing, is the agressive horseflies today. On the hike near Ft. Assiniboine the day before, the flies were just annoying; today they were absolutely vicious! The Muskol I was slathering on had no affect on them. I lost count at how many I killed while sucking on my bare arms (it was a hot day!), once the result being a hand full of the blood the thing had just sucked out of me.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Once near the cap, I made my way off the trail and UP for a short bit and found the tell tale guideposts in a small overgrown clearing. It was a real treat to stare at a pristine cap, something I'd been wanting to do for a couple of years now.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks for the cap that lured me out to experience this excellent little hike!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
      </groundspeak:logs>
      <groundspeak:travelbugs />
